## Step 1 — Set up and load the graph

`rdflib` is not pre-bundled with Pyodide, so it is installed with `micropip`.
HTTP has to go through `pyodide.http.pyfetch`: `requests` and `urllib.request`
do not work in the browser.

```{pyodide}
#| autorun: false
#| label: setup
#| output: false
import micropip
await micropip.install("rdflib=={{ rdflib_version }}")

from pyodide.http import pyfetch
from rdflib import Graph

# Deviation from the verbatim template (keep on re-sync): this graph dates
# events BC, so it carries xsd:gYear literals with negative years. rdflib tries
# to map them onto a Python datetime.date (minimum year 1), fails, and logs a
# traceback per literal. Nothing is wrong — the queries read the lexical form —
# but dozens of tracebacks in the browser console look alarming.
import logging
logging.getLogger("rdflib.term").setLevel(logging.ERROR)

GRAPH_URL = "{{ qmd.graph_url }}"

_cache = {}

async def load(url):
    """Fetch a Turtle file and parse it into a Graph. Cached per URL."""
    if url in _cache:
        return _cache[url]
    resp = await pyfetch(url)
    if resp.status != 200:
        raise RuntimeError(f"{url} -> HTTP {resp.status}")
    g = Graph()
    g.parse(data=await resp.string(), format="turtle")
    _cache[url] = g
    return g

def show(graph, query):
    """Run a query and return the rows as a list of dicts."""
    res = graph.query(PREFIXES + query)
    cols = [str(v) for v in res.vars]
    return [{c: (None if r[i] is None else str(r[i])) for i, c in enumerate(cols)}
            for r in res]

PREFIXES = """
{{ prefixes }}"""
{% if has_viz %}
# --- figures (deviation from the verbatim template; keep on re-sync) --------
# Some queries carry a `viz:` block in queries.yaml: a short Python file that
# turns that query's rows into a browser figure. Two small things make that
# work here.
#
# `results` keeps each query's rows under its id, so a figure cell can be
# re-run, or run after some other query, without the rows underneath it having
# silently changed.
#
# `Frame` renders a self-contained HTML document inside an iframe. It has to:
# quarto-live inserts cell output with `innerHTML`, and a `<script>` tag
# inserted that way never executes, whereas a browser does load an iframe's
# document after the element is in the DOM. The frame also keeps the figure's
# CSS and element ids away from the page, which is why the Jupyter version of
# these figures uses `IPython.display.IFrame` and a fresh uuid per cell.
import json
from html import escape

results = {}


class Frame:
    """A self-contained HTML document, shown inline as a figure."""

    def __init__(self, html, height=520):
        self.html = html
        self.height = height

    def _repr_html_(self):
        # Only these two need escaping inside a double-quoted attribute, and
        # the ampersand has to go first or it would escape the escapes.
        doc = self.html.replace("&", "&amp;").replace('"', "&quot;")
        return (f'<iframe srcdoc="{doc}" loading="lazy"'
                f' sandbox="allow-scripts allow-popups'
                f' allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox"'
                f' style="width:100%;height:{self.height}px;border:0">'
                f'</iframe>')

{{ qmd.viz_prelude_code }}
{% endif %}
```

